From fb9ebf36a028116067ab9140e721245a560c8ee0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Guido Urdaneta <guidou@chromium.org>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 00:23:42 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [Backport] CVE-2026-113161: Use after free in WebRTC
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Manual cherry-pick of patch originally reviewed on
https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8577741:
Do not optimize RTC encoded stream transfer if context is destroyed

When a ReadableStream created by RTCRtpReceiver::createEncodedStreams()
or RTCRtpSender::createEncodedStreams() is transferred and deserialized
in a destination ExecutionContext that has already been destroyed,
PerformInProcessOptimization() previously constructed a new
RTCEncoded{Audio,Video}UnderlyingSource on the destroyed context and
bound it to the active WebRTC encoded stream transformer. Because
NotifyContextDestroyed() had already fired on the destination context,
ContextDestroyed() would never be invoked to unregister the transformer
callback.

Mirroring https://crrev.com/c/8463165 (crbug.com/565742180), guard
RtcEncoded{Audio,Video}{Receiver,Sender}SourceOptimizer::
PerformInProcessOptimization() against a null or destroyed
ExecutionContext, invoking the disconnect callback before returning
nullptr so the encoded stream transformer callback is unregistered.
Also add a defensive guard to
RtcEncoded{Audio,Video}{Receiver,Sender}SinkOptimizer::
PerformInProcessOptimization().

Fixed: 568945639
Change-Id: I618e945c4c4a06c866be936b1a489bd5c109d5c5
